<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mostar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mostar is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neretva">Neretva</a> river. It grew under Ottoman rule around a bridge crossing, and its Old Bridge (Stari Most), completed in 1566, became the symbol of the city. The bridge was destroyed in 1993 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnian-war">Bosnian War</a> and rebuilt in 2004, and the old town was inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2005.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mostar.jpg" alt="Mostar" width="320" height="115" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stari_Most_viewed_from_North.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mostar stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neretva">Neretva</a> between Mount Hum and the Velež Mountain. People have lived on the river since prehistory, as fortified enclosures and cemeteries show, and there is evidence of Roman occupation beneath the present town. It is the historical capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> and the administrative centre of Herzegovina-Neretva Canton. The name comes from the mostari, the bridge-keepers who guarded the Old Bridge in the Ottoman era.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name Mostar first appears in a document of 1474, which refers to a wooden bridge used by traders, soldiers and others. The town was then also the seat of a kadiluk, a district with a regional judge. It lay on the trade route between the Adriatic and the mineral-rich regions of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, and the settlement spread to the right bank. The region came under Ottoman rule in 1468. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> called the place Köprühisar, 'fortress at the bridge', and organised it into the čaršija, the crafts and commercial centre, and the mahala, the residential area.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> in 1878 and held it until 1918, after which Mostar belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. In 1881 it became the seat of the Catholic diocese of Mostar-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomislavgrad">Duvno</a>. After Bosnia and Herzegovina declared independence in April 1992, the Yugoslav People's Army besieged the town following clashes with Croat forces, and Mostar became the capital of the Croatian Republic of Herzeg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>. The Old Bridge was destroyed in the war. Its reconstruction began in 1999 and was mostly complete by spring 2004, funded by donations from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Old Bridge, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, was commissioned by Suleiman in the 16th century and is considered an exemplary piece of Islamic architecture in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. The city also has an Italianate Franciscan church, the Ottoman Muslibegovića house, the Dalmatian Ćorović House and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> cathedral built as a gift from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mostar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From his castle at Blagaj near <b>Mostar</b>, Stjepan controlled the caravan routes between the Adriatic and the interior, levying customs on trade with Ragusa and developing the port of Novi, today Herceg Novi.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saint-sava">Duchy of Saint Sava</a></li></ul></section>
